ys E1, they flip my D1. So that seems neutral. If I play E1, the opponent plays D1, flipping my E1 AND E2 (because of a diagonal flip). Having to give up one piece versus two pieces is a crucial difference.\n\nAfter playing D1, E2 is a blank square, while if I play E1, E2 becomes an opponent piece. This gives the opponent more possibilities. So, I must pick the move that results in the least material loss and reduces the chances of the opponent making more moves. Therefore, the optimal move is D1.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The valid moves are (D, 1), (E, 1), (G, 1), and (G, 2).\n1.  **Moves (G, 1) and (G, 2)**: Both of these moves are adjacent to the empty corner H1.\n    *   Playing **(G, 1)** (a C-square) allows the opponent to play H1 immediately by sandwiching the new piece at G1 against the existing piece at F1 (H1 -> G1* -> F1@).\n    *   Playing **(G, 2)** (an X-square) flips F3 to *. This creates a diagonal chain allowing the opponent to play H1 immediately (H1 -> G2* -> F3* -> E4* -> D5@).\n    *   Giving the opponent a corner this early is usually a decisive strategic error. Thus, these moves should be avoided.\n\n2.  **Moves (D, 1) and (E, 1)**: These moves involve playing into a 2-square gap on the west edge (Column 1), between the opponent\'s pieces at C1 and F1.\n    *   In such "edge pair" scenarios, the opponent will typically respond by playing in the remaining empty square, capturing the piece you just played and securing that section of the edge.\n    *   If you play **(D, 1)**, the opponent responds with E1. This flips D1. It does not flip D2 or other internal pieces.\n    *   If you play **(E, 1)**, the opponent responds with D1. This flips E1 *and* creates a diagonal sandwich to flip E2 (D1 -> E2 -> F3).\n    *   Therefore, (D, 1) is the better choice as it minimizes the damage caused by the opponent\'s inevitable counter-move.\n\nReason: Moves (G, 1) and (G, 2) are rejected because they immediately allow the opponent to take the H1 corner